Keep QThread's prctl(PR_SET_NAME) call behind QT_LINUXBASE.

qthread_unix.cpp:46 includes <sys/prctl.h> only on
`defined(Q_OS_LINUX) && !defined(QT_LINUXBASE)`. Qt 6.10 guarded the
thread-name call the same way; Qt 6.12 dropped the QT_LINUXBASE half at
the call site (qthread_unix.cpp:353, lines as of Qt 6.12.0) and kept it
on the include, so a QT_LINUXBASE build names an undeclared PR_SET_NAME
and fails to compile.

This restores the 6.10 guard. A QT_LINUXBASE build falls through to the
`#else` branch and leaves the thread unnamed, as it did with Qt 6.10.2.

diff --git a/src/corelib/thread/qthread_unix.cpp b/src/corelib/thread/qthread_unix.cpp
index 36e34fd..aaa3a31 100644
--- a/src/corelib/thread/qthread_unix.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/thread/qthread_unix.cpp
@@ -350,7 +350,7 @@ template <typename String>
 static void setCurrentThreadName(QThread *thr, String &objectName)
 {
     auto setit = [](const char *name) {
-#  if defined(Q_OS_LINUX)
+#  if defined(Q_OS_LINUX) && !defined(QT_LINUXBASE)
         prctl(PR_SET_NAME, name);
 #  elif defined(Q_OS_DARWIN)
         pthread_setname_np(name);
